on/math-academy-id "q-3781" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2{x}^{3}-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-3781/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-3781"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e9db10c1-60c5-58be-abf3-51ce64715667" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528/selection/7bc54ef2cebd9c4cf6fef3072c83d13d0dfd6652d02cb9919fbcbc5497b7a408" :answer/id #uuid "9040d46e-4db2-5122-8530-d9e97777c6c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :answer/id #uuid "1a810eff-87d3-5517-b9c4-853087c35e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "1c4a2e46-69f5-5cfb-bef4-c9e1b633e1d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "2aab37f4-c1ce-5c35-a1e6-bbf628ed35a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "42b24e91-9b04-5f1c-a582-e36a4ce56aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115528/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :answer-field/choices ["q-115528/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-115528/selection/7bc54ef2cebd9c4cf6fef3072c83d13d0dfd6652d02cb9919fbcbc5497b7a408" "q-115528/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" "q-115528/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-115528/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115528" :question/id #uuid "115b44c6-02ff-530e-996f-805570ccd3cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-115528" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115528/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115528"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336802/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "e9c32464-f609-5091-80ba-b70256ac5e6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336802/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "158a9856-3ee6-5860-9f01-ea88bab1c692" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336802/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-336802/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336802" :question/id #uuid "808beaf8-4c4f-5366-8636-d03635acae03" :question/math-academy-id "q-336802" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)]}^{′}}{{[ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x})}{(-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336802/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336802"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336903/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "47ea25ec-db6b-58be-a50e-d5eac4a24611" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186259773 :answer-field/choices ["q-336903/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-336903"] :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{′}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{′}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-336927"] :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5){5}^{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5){e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
